#=GF RN [1]
#=GF RM 15220462
#=GF RT The 3' cis-acting genomic replication element of the severe acute
#=GF RT respiratory syndrome coronavirus can function in the murine coronavirus
#=GF RT genome.
#=GF RA Goebel SJ, Taylor J, Masters PS
#=GF RL J Virol. 2004;78:7846-7851.
#=GF RN [2]
#=GF RM 25736566
#=GF RT The structure and functions of coronavirus genomic 3' and 5' ends.
#=GF RA Yang D, Leibowitz JL
#=GF RL Virus Res. 2015;206:120-133.
#=GF RN [3]
#=GF RM 21378501
#=GF RT RNA-RNA and RNA-protein interactions in coronavirus replication and
#=GF RT transcription.
#=GF RA Sola I, Mateos-Gomez PA, Almazan F, Zuniga S, Enjuanes L
#=GF RL RNA Biol. 2011;8:237-248.
#=GF RN [4]
#=GF RM 27712622
#=GF RT Coronavirus cis-Acting RNA Elements.
#=GF RA Madhugiri R, Fricke M, Marz M, Ziebuhr J
#=GF RL Adv Virus Res. 2016;96:127-163.
#=GF CC The first 150 to 200 nucleotides within the 5' UTR of betacoronaviruses
#=GF CC are highly structured and shown to be conserved on a structure level among
#=GF CC betacoronaviruses. It has been predicted to contain 3 conserved stem-loops
#=GF CC (SL1, SL2 and SL4). SARS-CoV and BCoV have an additional stem-loop, called
#=GF CC SL3, which contains the TRS-L sequence in its loop region. However,
#=GF CC according to predictions, SL3 is not stable in other betacoronaviruses
#=GF CC like MHV. SL1 is important for viral replication, most likely playing a
#=GF CC role in the template-switch of viral subgenomic RNA transcription. SL2 is
#=GF CC crucial for viral viability, however, the nucleotide sequence can be
#=GF CC mutated and some residues, as long as the structure remains stable. SL4 is
#=GF CC hypothesized to play a role in directing subgenomic RNA synthesis during
#=GF CC viral replication.
#=GF WK Coronavirus_5'_UTR
#=GF SQ 17

